# Line endings are a property of the REPOSITORY, not of each machine's git
# config. Without a rule here the answer comes from `core.autocrlf`, which is
# per-clone and per-developer, so two people editing the same file disagree about
# what a line ending is — and the disagreement is invisible until a diff comes
# back as "every line changed" for a two-line edit, which is exactly how this was
# found.
#
# `text=auto` lets git decide what is text (binaries are left alone by content
# inspection, so no allow-list here can go stale); `eol=lf` then fixes the working
# tree to LF on every platform, Windows included. Stored content was already LF —
# `core.autocrlf` had been normalizing it on the way in — so this changes nothing
# that is committed, only what lands on disk at checkout.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Kept as its own row even though the rule above already covers it, because the
# consequence here is specific and worth stating: a CRLF shebang line makes the
# kernel look for an interpreter whose name ends in a carriage return, so a shell
# script that picked up CRLF does not misbehave — it fails to start at all.
*.sh text eol=lf
